#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# drop

#% text
# `drop(xs, n)` gives the list `xs` without its first `n` elements.

#% text
# `drop(xs, -n)` gives `xs` without its last `n` elements.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `n` must be an integer whose size is at most the length of `xs`.

#% item
# `drop(xs, 1)` is `rest(xs)`; `drop(xs, -1)` is `most(xs)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
drop([a, b, c, d, e], 2)
#% output
#= [c, d, e]

#% cell
drop([a, b, c, d, e], -2)
#% output
#= [a, b, c]

#% cell
drop([1, 2], 2)
#% output
#= []

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A header row removed from a table of data:

#% cell
rows = [["x", "y"], [1, 2.5], [2, 3.1]]
drop(rows, 1)
#% output
#= [[1, 2.5], [2, 3.1]]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The burn-in of a chain discarded before averaging:

#% cell
seed(9); chain = nest_list(x => 0.5*x + random_real([-1, 1]), 10.0, 200)
[mean(chain), mean(drop(chain, 50))]
#% output
#= [0.15378672430595883, 0.03174436173039171]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `drop` keeps what `take` leaves:

#% cell
xs = range(6)
[take(xs, 2), drop(xs, 2)]
#% output
#= [[1, 2], [3, 4, 5, 6]]

#% cell
drop(xs, -1) === most(xs)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Dropping more than there is:

#% cell
drop([1, 2], 3)
#% output
#! failure(index, 3, 2)

#% text
# To remove failures from a list, use `drop_failures`, not `drop`:

#% cell
drop_failures([1, failure(no_root, find_root), 3])
#% output
#= [1, 3]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `take`, `rest`, `most`, `drop_failures`, `select`
